**Proof.**

Let $\alpha=\angle A$, $\beta=\angle B$, $\gamma=\angle C$. Place $I$ at the origin and let $AI$ be the positive $x$-axis, with $A=(a,0)$, $a=AI>0$. Since the given circle is tangent to $AI$ at $I$, its center lies on the line through $I$ perpendicular to $AI$; write it as $(0,\rho)$ ($\rho>0$, with $B$ above the $x$-axis). The circle has equation

$$x^{2}+y^{2}-2\rho y=0. \tag{1}$$

---

**Angles at $I$.** Since $I$ is the incenter, $\angle AIB=90^{\circ}+\gamma/2$ and $\angle AIC=90^{\circ}+\beta/2$. Hence the ray $IB$ makes angle $90^{\circ}+\gamma/2$ with the positive $x$-axis.

**Point $B$.** Writing $B=t(-\sin(\gamma/2),\cos(\gamma/2))$ and substituting into (1) gives $t=2\rho\cos(\gamma/2)$, so

$$B=\rho(-\sin\gamma,\;1+\cos\gamma). \tag{2}$$

**Points $P$ and $Q$ (tangent–chord theorem).** The tangent to the circle at $I$ is the line $AI$ (the $x$-axis). Since $P\in AB$, the inscribed angle $\angle IBP=\angle IBA=\beta/2$. By the tangent–chord theorem applied to chord $IP$,

$$\angle AIP=\tfrac12\beta.$$

Since $Q\in BC$, $\angle IBQ=\angle IBC=\beta/2$. Applying the tangent–chord theorem to chord $IQ$ (the angle on the side containing $B$ is the supplement, since $B$ lies on the major-arc side of chord $IQ$) gives

$$\angle AIQ=180^{\circ}-\tfrac12\beta.$$

(Consistency: $\angle PIQ=\angle AIQ-\angle AIP=180^{\circ}-\beta=180^{\circ}-\angle PBQ$, confirming $P,B,Q,I$ are concyclic.)

A ray from $I$ at angle $\theta$ (with $\sin\theta>0$) meets circle (1) again at $2\rho\sin\theta\,(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)$. Thus

$$P=2\rho\sin\tfrac{\beta}{2}\bigl(\cos\tfrac{\beta}{2},\sin\tfrac{\beta}{2}\bigr)=\rho(\sin\beta,\;1-\cos\beta),$$

$$Q=2\rho\sin\tfrac{\beta}{2}\bigl(-\cos\tfrac{\beta}{2},\sin\tfrac{\beta}{2}\bigr)=\rho(-\sin\beta,\;1-\cos\beta).$$

So $P,Q$ are mirror images across the $y$-axis, and

$$|PI|=2\rho\sin\tfrac{\beta}{2},\qquad |PI|^{2}=4\rho^{2}\sin^{2}\tfrac{\beta}{2}. \tag{3}$$

**Length $|BQ|$.** From (2) and the formula for $Q$,

$$|BQ|^{2}=\rho^{2}(\sin\gamma-\sin\beta)^{2}+\rho^{2}(\cos\beta+\cos\gamma)^{2}=2\rho^{2}\bigl(1+\cos(\beta+\gamma)\bigr)=4\rho^{2}\cos^{2}\tfrac{\beta+\gamma}{2}.$$

Since $\beta+\gamma=180^{\circ}-\alpha$, $\cos\frac{\beta+\gamma}{2}=\sin\frac{\alpha}{2}$, hence

$$|BQ|=2\rho\sin\tfrac{\alpha}{2}. \tag{4}$$

**Length $a=AI$.** The ray $AB$ from $A$ makes angle $180^{\circ}-\alpha/2$ with the positive $x$-axis, so $B-A$ is parallel to $(-\cos\frac{\alpha}{2},\,\sin\frac{\alpha}{2})$. Using $B-A=(-\rho\sin\gamma-a,\;\rho(1+\cos\gamma))$,

$$\frac{\rho(1+\cos\gamma)}{-\rho\sin\gamma-a}=-\tan\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\;\Longrightarrow\;a=\rho\,\frac{1+\cos\gamma}{\tan(\alpha/2)}-\rho\sin\gamma.$$

Simplifying with $1+\cos\gamma=2\cos^{2}\frac{\gamma}{2}$, $\sin\gamma=2\sin\frac{\gamma}{2}\cos\frac{\gamma}{2}$:

$$a=2\rho\cos\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\,\frac{\cos\frac{\gamma}{2}\cos\frac{\alpha}{2}-\sin\frac{\gamma}{2}\sin\frac{\alpha}{2}}{\sin\frac{\alpha}{2}}=2\rho\cos\tfrac{\gamma}{2}\,\frac{\cos\frac{\alpha+\gamma}{2}}{\sin\frac{\alpha}{2}}=\frac{2\rho\cos(\gamma/2)\,\sin(\beta/2)}{\sin(\alpha/2)}, \tag{5}$$

since $\frac{\alpha+\gamma}{2}=90^{\circ}-\frac{\beta}{2}$.

**Point $R$ and length $|AR|$.** The line $IQ$ has direction $(-\sin\beta,\,1-\cos\beta)$, so its points are $t(-\sin\beta,\,1-\cos\beta)$. The line $AC$ issues from $A$ in direction $(-\cos\frac{\alpha}{2},-\sin\frac{\alpha}{2})$ (angle $180^{\circ}+\alpha/2$), so its points are $(a,0)+\mu(-\cos\frac{\alpha}{2},-\sin\frac{\alpha}{2})$. Equating:

$$t(-\sin\beta,\,1-\cos\beta)=\bigl(a-\mu\cos\tfrac{\alpha}{2},\;-\mu\sin\tfrac{\alpha}{2}\bigr).$$

From the $y$-component, $\mu=-t(1-\cos\beta)/\sin\frac{\alpha}{2}$; substituting into the $x$-component and using $1-\cos\beta=2\sin^{2}\frac{\beta}{2}$, $\sin\beta=2\sin\frac{\beta}{2}\cos\frac{\beta}{2}$:

$$t=-\frac{a\sin(\alpha/2)}{2\sin(\beta/2)\cos(\gamma/2)}.$$

Since the direction of $AC$ is a unit vector, $|AR|=|\mu|$. Using (5),

$$|AR|=|\mu|=\frac{|t|\,(1-\cos\beta)}{\sin(\alpha/2)}=\frac{a\sin(\alpha/2)}{2\sin(\beta/2)\cos(\gamma/2)}\cdot\frac{2\sin^{2}(\beta/2)}{\sin(\alpha/2)}=\frac{a\sin(\beta/2)}{\cos(\gamma/2)}=\frac{2\rho\sin^{2}(\beta/2)}{\sin(\alpha/2)}. \tag{6}$$

**Conclusion.** Multiplying (4) and (6),

$$|AR|\cdot|BQ|=\frac{2\rho\sin^{2}(\beta/2)}{\sin(\alpha/2)}\cdot 2\rho\sin\frac{\alpha}{2}=4\rho^{2}\sin^{2}\frac{\beta}{2}=|PI|^{2},$$

by (3). $\blacksquare$

